[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPSIB-9
TITLE: PC Communication Interface
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the PC via the microcontroller's serial UART interface. The
firmware both receives and transmits data on this UART port. Due to the nature of UART having
independent transmit and receive lines, the transmit and receive functionality operate
independently. As the UART baud rate is relatively slow compared to the processing speed of the
microcontroller, both sending and receiving data is performed with non-blocking interrupt callback
functions.

Transmitted data is sent as a buffer of bytes, representing a singular data packet to the PC, with
the interrupt callback occurring once all data bytes have been transmitted. The firmware sets a flag
when transmission is initiated, and clears the flag after transmission is finished, to not create
transmission conflicts on the transmit line.

Reception is performed one byte at a time, with the interrupt callback occurring once a data byte is
available. The parsing of the received byte is done to determine whether a complete data packet
has been received from the PC, as according to the communication protocol. Once a completed
data packet has been received, the firmware sets a flag for further data processing outside of the
parsing inside of the interrupt callback.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPSIB-2
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPSIB-10
TITLE: PC Communication Processing
STATEMENT: >>>
The communication protocol the firmware employs for communicating with the PC utilizes ASCII
character bytes. The firmware both sends and receives data packets. Each received data packet
from the PC begins with a asterisk character ('*') and terminates with a newline character ('\n'). The
value of the bytes in between these are comprised of a fixed length command, and a variable
length data payload. Both command and data payload are also represented by ASCII character
bytes.

There are four different valid commands that the PC can send: ping, erase, write, and verify. Each
command is represented in the transmitted packet by preset 5-character values. The firmware
compares the received command to these preset values. There is no associated data payload for
the ping and erase command. The' write' command has a data payload containing the flash page
index and the values to be written to said flash page. The verify command has a CRC value as its
data payload. The data payload for both commands are sent as ASCII characters which are
formatted as human readable hexadecimal values.

Each transmitted data packet from the firmware to the PC begins with a period character ('.') and
terminates with a newline character ('\n'). The value of the bytes in between are a fixed length
response. The fixed length response are 13 ASCII characters which correspond to the specific
incoming command that is being responded to (e.g. ping, erase, write, and verify).
<<<
